<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=calculus">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Calculus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1665">1665</a>–<a href="/years/1684">1684</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Infinitesimal calculus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Calculus is the branch of mathematics that studies continuous change, formulated independently by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</a> in 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its two branches, differential and integral calculus, study rates of change and the accumulation of quantities. Newton derived his results in 1665–1666, but Leibniz published first, in 1684, and the dispute over credit divided English-speaking from continental European mathematicians for many years. Newton applied its methods to planetary motion in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a> (1687), while much of the notation still in use comes from Leibniz.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/calculus.jpg" alt="Calculus" width="320" height="473" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: G. W. Leibniz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leibniz-Acta-1684-NovaMethodus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1665–1666</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Europe</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd><a href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Newton derived his results in 1665–1666 but Leibniz published first, in 1684; they reached them independently, though the credit was bitterly disputed.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/euclids-elements">Euclid's Elements<span>c. 300 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/algebra">Algebra<span>825</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Laws of motion and gravitation<span>1687</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=calculus">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
